@flexiarg cascade-construction/add-a-pattern-when-an-item-fits-no-class
@title Add A Pattern When A Real Item Fits No Existing Class
@keywords gap, unclassifiable, new-pattern, operator-distinction, extension
@audience agents constructing cascades, library authors
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-17 (claude-7; generated-by-role, 2026-09-16)
@references [hygiene/classify-by-remedy-judgement cascade-construction/run-it-on-a-real-case inbox-zero/generated-by-role]
@see-also apparatus/new-failure-class-is-a-design-defect
@why when a real item in the run fits none of the cascade's classes, either the item is exceptional or the cascade is missing a step. Forcing it into the nearest class hides the second case; stopping to name the gap and ask the person who knows the domain usually finds a distinction the requirement never wrote down.
@how when running on a real case, list items no pattern acts on; name the class they form and check whether the requirement covers it; if not, draft a pattern and bring the distinctions it needs to someone who knows the domain; write the pattern from those distinctions, grounded in a census of real items, not the first example.
@violation-signature an unclassified item filed under the nearest class; a new class defined by file extension or keyword; a new pattern built from a single example without a census.
! conclusion: A real item that fits no class becomes a named gap, a domain question and, when warranted, a pattern built from a census.

  + context: A cascade is being run on real state.

  + IF:
    Some items are acted on by no pattern.

  + HOWEVER:
    The nearest class usually looks close enough.

  + THEN:
    Name the gap, ask about the distinction, census the real items, and add the pattern.

  + BECAUSE:
    The distinction the requirement missed is often one the operator already makes without having said it.

  + evidence: p4ng's nine tracked generated files fit none of inbox zero's three dirt classes; Joe's distinction (".aux we should probably never commit; .pdf sometimes we should") and a census (p4ng 40 tracked build byproducts, futon2's 268 tracked .log files that are run cassettes) produced generated-by-role with four roles (futon3 56ccd8f).
